A||N||3||Two88GTs||Found a great auto tranny pan gasket||||14||
Z||000000||Two88GTs||12-10-2001||11:14 PM||||Synopsis: A shadetree mechanic/Fiero owner(SM/FO) seeks to fix a leaking ATF problem.<P>Diagnosis: 13 year old pan gasket broken in several places with RTV smeared unevenly is the culprit behind the leak.(previous SM/FO)<P>Attempted cure #1: SM/FO goes to AutoZone and buys a filter/gasket kit for about $17.  Goes home to find that the gasket is made out of cork.  CORK!!!  SM/FO changes the filter and gasket anyways and checks for leaks.  Leak is still there.  Pulls pan, finds broken gasket. Swears a lot. <P>Attempted cure #2: SM/FO goes to local GM dealer and buys the Delco OEM replacement 8678168 for $10.35(GM wholesale).  SM/FO notices a bead of RTV built-in on the gasket which seems to be a nice touch.  SM/FO changes gasket.  Drives car and checks for leaks.  Leak is still there.  Pulls pan, finds gasket intact.  SM/FO knows he tightened the bolts to correct specs.  Suspects pan is not quite flat but thinks Delco gasket is too thin.  Swears a lot.<BR>BTW, SM/FO is too lazy and stupid to pound pan flat.<P>Attempted cure #3: SM/FO goes to a small parts store buys a gasket made by ATP for about $6.  Goes home to find that the gasket is at least twice the thickness of the OEM gasket and the gasket material is pliable enough to come folded in a box(gasket unfolds flat).  SM/FO changes the gasket in 40 degree weather hours before six inches of snow.  Drives and checks for leaks.  Leak is gone.  Yells, "Woo Hoo!" many many times.<P>Now, the AC Delco gasket worked well on my other car so I'm guessing that the pan's just not as flat as my other one, BUT the ATP gasket seems to be a better product and is 1/2 the price of the Delco gasket.<P>Model # for the THC-125C is XG-1 for the ATP gasket.  I hope the needlessly long story above proves useful to someone.||||reg||14
Z||000001||rodmcneill||12-11-2001||12:13 AM||||Thanks for the info, that is a messy job.||||reg||14
